WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:09.542
How many of you have ever walked 
into a room and wondered,

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:13.313
“Why did I come here in the first place?”

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:16.707
Or maybe you left something behind, 
your pencil case, for example,

00:00:16.707 --> 00:00:19.275
and you have to circle the entire house

00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:22.437
before coming back to the place 
where you originally started

00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:24.696
only to find it sitting right there?

00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:27.606
Anyone had any similar experiences?

00:00:27.606 --> 00:00:28.467
Great.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:31.605
Fo me, it’s things like 
this that define my life.

00:00:31.605 --> 00:00:38.096
My friends know me for continuously losing
my water bottle, among other things.

00:00:38.096 --> 00:00:41.740
I am, in every sense of the word, 
a scatterbrain.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.288
And I was feeling pretty foolish 
about it myself

00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:48.176
until I read this article 
about this book called,

00:00:48.176 --> 00:00:52.046
“Where Good Ideas Come From: 
The Natural History of Innovation."

00:00:52.046 --> 00:00:57.877
And in it, the author Steven Johnson 
compares settlements of different types,

00:00:57.877 --> 00:00:59.777
so like cities and towns,

00:00:59.777 --> 00:01:06.286
noting that as cities get bigger,
they generate ideas at a faster clip.

00:01:06.286 --> 00:01:12.197
Why? Because of the increase in the number
of ideas in a fixed area.

00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:15.498
So in our context, 
a scatterbrain can be seen

00:01:15.498 --> 00:01:19.507
as a metropolis with bits 
of disorganized information

00:01:19.507 --> 00:01:23.666
floating around, 
constantly crashing into each other.

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:25.088
It's messy.

00:01:25.088 --> 00:01:30.117
But it is also more likely 
for two unrelated ideas to collide

00:01:30.117 --> 00:01:32.927
giving rise to new innovations,

00:01:32.927 --> 00:01:35.080
like hip-hop – ballet fusion

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.437
or the use of math to formulate 
a harmonic melody.

00:01:39.437 --> 00:01:42.608
So in essence, a scatterbrain then,

00:01:42.608 --> 00:01:46.159
becomes a source 
of creativity and innovation

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:49.226
and therefore, 
it's just as Steven Johnson said:

00:01:49.226 --> 00:01:53.615
“The more disorganized your brain is, 
the smarter you are.”

00:01:53.615 --> 00:01:59.436
Now, considering that I had the potential 
of being the queen of scatterbrains,

00:01:59.436 --> 00:02:02.108
I was feeling pretty good about myself.

00:02:02.108 --> 00:02:05.549
But another part of me
started to wonder,

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:11.675
“Hold up. If you’re such a genius then how
come none of your ideas ever take shape?”

00:02:11.675 --> 00:02:16.977
And that brings us to the greatest
disaster faced by all scatterbrains:

00:02:16.977 --> 00:02:18.877
flooding.

00:02:18.877 --> 00:02:22.776
It happens internally and externally. 
Let me explain.

00:02:22.776 --> 00:02:24.406
Now, I don’t know about you,

00:02:24.406 --> 00:02:30.717
but 90% of the best ideas I have ever had
have come to me when I’m in the shower.

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:34.697
So, I might have an English Literature 
essay due the next day,

00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:39.767
and I might be shampooing my hair when all
of a sudden this line pops up in my head

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:43.996
"Despite his high status, 
Othello, the tragic hero,

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:49.276
eventually falls to his doom due 
to jealousy – his greatest hamartia."

00:02:49.276 --> 00:02:53.358
And I'm like "Yes! This will make
a top-notch literature sentence."

00:02:53.358 --> 00:02:55.226
So I quickly finish showering,

00:02:55.226 --> 00:02:56.306
get my clothes on,

00:02:56.306 --> 00:02:59.340
seat myself at my table, 
pen and paper in hand,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.239
ready to write that golden essay.

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:03.737
And I reach into my head to find that line

00:03:03.737 --> 00:03:08.047
only to find lyrics 
to a Michael Jackson song

00:03:08.047 --> 00:03:11.096
or images from the last movie I watched.

00:03:11.096 --> 00:03:13.348
In the end I’m just left there thinking,

00:03:13.348 --> 00:03:19.628
“Hmm, I had this really nice way of saying
Othello dies. What was it again?"

00:03:19.628 --> 00:03:23.695
My mind is so flooded that I can’t 
find that sentence

00:03:23.695 --> 00:03:27.066
I came up with just two minutes ago!

00:03:27.066 --> 00:03:30.278
The second type of flooding 
happens externally

00:03:30.278 --> 00:03:32.856
by people putting things in your brain.

00:03:32.856 --> 00:03:36.607
So, for example, I might be 
on my way to school and my mom would say,

00:03:36.607 --> 00:03:40.396
"Hey, why don't you buy this chocolate
from this particular shop

00:03:40.396 --> 00:03:43.541
because apparently, 
it tastes really good."

00:03:43.541 --> 00:03:48.416
And then I'd go to school 
and I'd get attacked

00:03:48.416 --> 00:03:51.036
by an arsenal of math equations.

00:03:51.036 --> 00:03:52.396
And then I might go to lunch

00:03:52.396 --> 00:03:54.797
and my friends would give 
me this list of movies

00:03:54.797 --> 00:03:56.907
to remember to watch over the weekend.

00:03:56.907 --> 00:03:59.868
And the day would go on and on 
with things piling in my head

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:02.837
until finally, 
it looked something like this.

00:04:02.837 --> 00:04:05.879
This, my friends, is what 
a scatterbrain looks like.

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:11.846
All those pieces of paper are bits of 
information that people put in your brain.

00:04:11.846 --> 00:04:15.517
And sometimes they go in, 
sometimes they don’t.

00:04:15.517 --> 00:04:20.547
And in this mess, the chances of 
remembering to look at the information

00:04:20.547 --> 00:04:26.068
that says “remember your bottle,” 
well, that's pretty slim.

00:04:26.068 --> 00:04:29.183
Yet, strangely enough,
my memory becomes flawless

00:04:29.183 --> 00:04:32.665
when it came to things like remembering 
dialogues from a movie,

00:04:32.665 --> 00:04:34.716
or random facts,

00:04:34.716 --> 00:04:38.711
for example, did you know that 
the word for pineapple

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.287
in a lot of the languages of the world

00:04:41.287 --> 00:04:44.099
sound something like “ananas?"

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:47.054
So, for example, in Hindi, its “ananas,"

00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:48.637
in French its “ananas,",

00:04:48.637 --> 00:04:51.466
in Indonesian it's “nanas."

00:04:51.466 --> 00:04:53.138
It's a completely useless fact,

00:04:53.138 --> 00:04:58.024
like I'm probably never going to use it,
maybe I might, I don't know,

00:04:58.024 --> 00:05:02.705
but I remember it super clearly.

00:05:02.705 --> 00:05:04.906
And that got me thinking, "Why?"

00:05:04.906 --> 00:05:08.684
Why is it I can remember 
the translation for pineapple,

00:05:08.684 --> 00:05:13.447
but I can’t remember a sentence 
I came up with just two minutes ago?

00:05:13.447 --> 00:05:17.273
Forget that, I can’t even remember 
not to leave my water bottle behind!

00:05:17.273 --> 00:05:21.738
So after a lot of frustration 
and contemplation,

00:05:21.738 --> 00:05:26.277
I was able to condense it down
into three main points:

00:05:26.277 --> 00:05:27.617
sequence,

00:05:27.617 --> 00:05:28.797
association,

00:05:28.797 --> 00:05:30.677
and iteration,

00:05:30.677 --> 00:05:32.167
S.A.I.

00:05:32.167 --> 00:05:33.438
What does it mean?

00:05:33.438 --> 00:05:37.677
Well, sequence is your ability to find
a logical pattern

00:05:37.677 --> 00:05:39.878
in the information 
you have to remember.

00:05:39.878 --> 00:05:43.316
Association is when you link that
information with something

00:05:43.316 --> 00:05:47.387
weird, striking, memorable
to make it stick in your brain,

00:05:47.387 --> 00:05:50.150
and iteration is when you repeat it
so many times

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:53.027
that it automatically
gets stuck in your head.

00:05:53.027 --> 00:05:58.857
That's why when I go, 
"Hello, can you hear me?"

00:05:58.857 --> 00:06:01.778
I bet half of the people in this room
are able to sing along

00:06:01.778 --> 00:06:04.778
because they've heard it 1,000 times.

00:06:04.778 --> 00:06:06.457
Or when I say the word "frozen,"

00:06:06.457 --> 00:06:09.078
you immediately think of a blonde girl 
in a blue dress

00:06:09.078 --> 00:06:13.015
singing, "Let it go, let it go."

00:06:13.015 --> 00:06:15.766
Your brain is able to pick up 
that specific information

00:06:15.766 --> 00:06:18.547
and display it at the right moment.

00:06:18.547 --> 00:06:21.207
So that's for things we don't really
need to remember.

00:06:21.207 --> 00:06:23.877
How about the things 
that are really required?

00:06:23.877 --> 00:06:26.575
Well, we apply the same three rules.

00:06:26.575 --> 00:06:29.687
In fact, man has been 
using it throughout history.

00:06:29.687 --> 00:06:33.117
Before the time of writing, 
there was the oral tradition

00:06:33.117 --> 00:06:39.426
where the history of civilizations 
was passed down through songs and hymns.

00:06:39.426 --> 00:06:41.797
Forget history, think about yourself!

00:06:41.797 --> 00:06:45.577
Back in nursery, 
your teachers used nursery rhymes

00:06:45.577 --> 00:06:49.147
to teach you things like alphabets, 
parts of the body, numbers.

00:06:49.147 --> 00:06:52.217
All these rhymes had sequence,

00:06:52.217 --> 00:06:54.889
could be associated 
with something interesting,

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:57.207
and if it was iterated enough times,

00:06:57.207 --> 00:06:59.997
it will automatically 
get stuck in your head.

00:06:59.997 --> 00:07:05.147
The first time I was formally introduced 
to memory techniques

00:07:05.147 --> 00:07:06.697
was when I was in sixth grade,

00:07:06.697 --> 00:07:11.167
and I was going through a phase
where I had somehow gotten it into my head

00:07:11.167 --> 00:07:15.727
that I could not continue living
unless I learned Japanese.

00:07:15.727 --> 00:07:19.541
So one day, I was in the kitchen with 
my mom who was cooking,

00:07:19.541 --> 00:07:24.137
and I was trying to learn the numbers
one to ten in Japanese.

00:07:24.137 --> 00:07:27.968
Emphasis on the word trying.

00:07:27.968 --> 00:07:29.816
I.e. I was going nowhere.

00:07:29.816 --> 00:07:32.617
But I wasn't about to give up
anytime soon,

00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:34.196
so I was rambling on and on,

00:07:34.196 --> 00:07:37.035
and my mom must have gotten fed up
because at one point,

00:07:37.035 --> 00:07:40.037
she gently interrupted me
and she was like,

00:07:40.037 --> 00:07:43.936
"Trishna, why don't you make
a story out of it?"

00:07:43.936 --> 00:07:49.666
And I was like, 'Mother, how the heck am
I supposed to make a story out of this?"

00:07:49.666 --> 00:07:52.297
And she was like, 
"Okay, think of it this way.

00:07:52.297 --> 00:07:57.856
One in Japanese is ichi and two is ni.

00:07:57.856 --> 00:08:02.087
Together, they sound something like
an itchy knee."

00:08:02.087 --> 00:08:04.727
And I was like, "Okay, 
I see what you did there."

00:08:04.727 --> 00:08:07.676
And I went ahead
and I created a story of my own.

00:08:07.676 --> 00:08:09.777
And it goes something like this:

00:08:09.777 --> 00:08:13.245
Once upon a time, 
there was a kid named Meg.

00:08:13.245 --> 00:08:16.306
Now Meg, had a very "ichi ni."

00:08:16.306 --> 00:08:21.207
And it was a very "san"ny day, 
so she was feeling really sleepy

00:08:21.207 --> 00:08:24.067
and she began to "yon" a lot.

00:08:24.067 --> 00:08:28.447
So to wake herself up, she decided
to "go" out and play,

00:08:28.447 --> 00:08:32.576
so she went outside and she started
playing with some "rokus,"

00:08:32.576 --> 00:08:35.527
but then she decided "nana,"

00:08:35.527 --> 00:08:39.827
I've got to stop because it had begun
raining and she had caught a cold

00:08:39.827 --> 00:08:42.667
and she was sneezing like, "hachi!"

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:46.987
So, she went to find shelter in 
the nearest store, but when she got there,

00:08:46.987 --> 00:08:49.906
she found a really long "kyuu,"

00:08:49.906 --> 00:08:52.397
but the shop owner who was this "jew"

00:08:52.397 --> 00:08:55.506
and he was really nice 
and he let her go in first.

00:08:55.506 --> 00:08:57.034
The end.

00:08:57.034 --> 00:09:03.125
It's a completely weird useless story
but that's kind of what made it stick.

00:09:03.125 --> 00:09:04.947
There was a sequence of events,

00:09:04.947 --> 00:09:08.378
I associated each word with a homophone,

00:09:08.378 --> 00:09:14.017
and if I repeat it enough times, it will
automatically get stuck in my brain.

00:09:14.017 --> 00:09:16.108
Perfect, right?

00:09:16.108 --> 00:09:19.024
With a technique like this,
I could remember anything!

00:09:19.024 --> 00:09:21.428
Unfortunately, no.

00:09:21.428 --> 00:09:26.576
I couldn't remember things when I didn't
remember that I had something to remember,

00:09:26.576 --> 00:09:28.617
my water bottle, for instance.

00:09:28.617 --> 00:09:31.086
So while I was excelling in class,

00:09:31.086 --> 00:09:34.856
where I had to remember things 
like lists and definitions,

00:09:34.856 --> 00:09:37.887
outside, I was still a complete mess.

00:09:37.887 --> 00:09:44.477
And then, when I entered junior high,
the BBC Sherlock Holmes TV show came out,

00:09:44.477 --> 00:09:51.326
and its protagonist, Sherlock Holmes,
had the one thing I had always longed for,

00:09:51.326 --> 00:09:54.527
an awesome memory.

00:09:54.527 --> 00:09:56.937
He called it his "mind palace."

00:09:56.937 --> 00:09:59.626
He could do this thing 
where within a matter of seconds,

00:09:59.626 --> 00:10:02.418
he could come out with the exact date, 
place and time

00:10:02.418 --> 00:10:05.008
a murder happened 20 years ago.

00:10:05.008 --> 00:10:06.558
I was blown away.

00:10:06.558 --> 00:10:11.968
So I did the first thing any girl 
of my generation would do; I Googled him.

00:10:11.968 --> 00:10:16.687
And I found out that his memory technique 
was quite a commonly used one

00:10:16.687 --> 00:10:19.098
called the Method of Loci.

00:10:19.098 --> 00:10:22.677
It uses what's known as spatial memory,

00:10:22.677 --> 00:10:27.456
which is your ability to remember 
geographical spaces in detail.

00:10:27.456 --> 00:10:30.877
So, for example, you know every
nook and cranny in your room,

00:10:30.877 --> 00:10:32.607
or maybe something bigger,

00:10:32.607 --> 00:10:35.747
you know all the roads in a certain area.

00:10:35.747 --> 00:10:40.546
So when I found out I was ecstatic 
and I wanted to put it to immediate use.

00:10:40.546 --> 00:10:44.137
And the most current problem 
I was having at the time

00:10:44.137 --> 00:10:47.215
was my tendency to lose things 
I was carrying with me,

00:10:47.215 --> 00:10:49.256
my water bottle, for instance.

00:10:49.256 --> 00:10:53.798
So I developed a routine wherein
before leaving a place,

00:10:53.798 --> 00:10:58.048
I would take a walk around school
in my head.

00:10:58.048 --> 00:10:59.876
What do I mean?

00:10:59.876 --> 00:11:03.387
Well, the first step to creating
a mind palace

00:11:03.387 --> 00:11:07.396
is picking a location 
you are really familiar with,

00:11:07.396 --> 00:11:10.689
and for me, personally, 
I know all the rooms in my school,

00:11:10.689 --> 00:11:13.607
so I made the school my mind palace.

00:11:13.607 --> 00:11:19.048
And I associated each room
with objects I tended to lose a lot.

00:11:19.048 --> 00:11:23.275
So, for example, the calculator
I would associate with the math room,

00:11:23.275 --> 00:11:25.255
my USB with the computer room,

00:11:25.255 --> 00:11:29.098
and my bottle with the lost and found,
and on and on,

00:11:29.098 --> 00:11:36.044
So before leaving a room, I would mentally
take a walk around each of these rooms

00:11:36.044 --> 00:11:40.498
and check to see if I had the associated 
object on me at the time.

00:11:40.498 --> 00:11:45.018
So for example, I would first go to the
math room associated with the calculator-

00:11:45.018 --> 00:11:48.247
Do I have it? Yes, and then move on
the next rooms

00:11:48.247 --> 00:11:50.162
checking to see if I had each object

00:11:50.162 --> 00:11:54.436
until finally I reached the lost and found
associated with the bottle-

00:11:54.436 --> 00:11:56.956
Do I have the bottle? Yes. Complete.

00:11:56.956 --> 00:12:00.544
Obviously, this is a highly simplified
version of the actual thing,

00:12:00.544 --> 00:12:03.786
but it's one way 
of putting the technique to use.

00:12:03.786 --> 00:12:06.383
And here, too, we can see S.A.I.

00:12:06.383 --> 00:12:08.326
We can see the sequence of rooms,

00:12:08.326 --> 00:12:12.336
and how each room is associated
with an object,

00:12:12.336 --> 00:12:16.137
and if I walk through these rooms
enough times,

00:12:16.137 --> 00:12:19.176
all of it will automatically get stuck
in my head.

00:12:19.176 --> 00:12:22.946
So that's the bottle problem solved.

00:12:22.946 --> 00:12:27.717
And I also manage
to retain my scatterbrain genius

00:12:27.717 --> 00:12:33.156
of being able to find anything I need
in the metropolitan mess of my mind

00:12:33.156 --> 00:12:35.586
using these memory techniques.

00:12:35.586 --> 00:12:37.648
So let's come back to the present.

00:12:37.648 --> 00:12:39.667
Hello everyone.

00:12:39.667 --> 00:12:44.366
My name's Trishna, I am 17-years old,
and I am a complete scatterbrain,

00:12:44.366 --> 00:12:51.126
but I manage my life pretty well using
my new and refined memory techniques.

00:12:51.126 --> 00:12:55.715
Well, it's not 100% foolproof,
but at least I can say with confidence

00:12:55.715 --> 00:12:59.197
I will never have to ask myself
this question again.

00:12:59.197 --> 00:13:00.797
Thank you.

